Two changes to public_html/api/deploy.php on wainkw.com.

Apply on the server, from the domain directory:

    cd ~/domains/wainkw.com
    cp public_html/api/deploy.php public_html/api/deploy.php.bak
    patch -p0 public_html/api/deploy.php < deploy-endpoint.patch

The endpoint is not in this repository, only this patch is, because nothing in
this environment can write to that server: the `hosa` connector's file access
is read-only, its one upload path is a TUS URL on a host the sandbox gateway
refuses at CONNECT, and www.wainkw.com is refused the same way. Every line
below was read out of the live file with `hosting_getWebsiteFileContentV1` on
10 September, so the context should match exactly.


WHY 1 — the endpoint refuses this site's own export

PROTECTED_PATHS is checked against the first path segment of every entry in the
artifact, and rejects the whole archive with `artifact_touches_protected_path`
before anything is downloaded. It lists `admin`, `queue`, `orders` and
`.htaccess`. All four are wain's: /admin/, /queue/ and /orders/ are static
routes this site publishes, and the .htaccess in the docroot is byte-identical
to the one in the export — 15672 bytes, header "Wain — static export on Apache
/ LiteSpeed (Hostinger)". So the endpoint cannot deploy this site at all. It
stops at the first of the four.

WHY 2 — and it does not protect five directories that do need protecting

Measured by listing the docroot and diffing it against the export. Everything
in the export is wain's; everything else in the docroot belongs to the older
PHP application:

  wain publishes   .htaccess 404 404.html _next about add admin apple-icon.png
                   brand build.json explore icon.svg index.html index.txt
                   manifest.webmanifest og og.jpg orders places privacy queue
                   robots.txt search sitemap.xml sw.js voice

  the PHP app has  api assets cats fonts hero images knet pay

`assets`, `cats`, `fonts`, `hero` and `images` are the PHP app's and are not on
the protected list, so an artifact containing any of them would overwrite that
application's files and the manifest prune would then delete them on the next
deploy. That is the quieter of the two faults and the more expensive one.

The replacement list is exactly "what the PHP app owns", plus the paths that
should never be written from an artifact under any circumstances.

.htaccess is deliberately NOT protected any more. It is wain's file, it is in
the export, and protecting it means the site can never correct its own caching
or routing rules through this endpoint. It is also the single highest-blast-
radius file in the docroot, since Apache reads it for both applications, so
`npm run audit:htaccess` checking every deny rule against every shipped file
stays the thing that has to be green before a deploy.


--- public_html/api/deploy.php
+++ public_html/api/deploy.php
@@
 const PROTECTED_PATHS = [
-    'api', 'knet', 'pay', 'admin', 'queue', 'orders',
-    'storage', 'cgi-bin', '.well-known',
-    '.htaccess', 'config.php',
+    // The older PHP application's directories. Measured against the docroot
+    // on 2026-09-10 by diffing it with the static export: everything here is
+    // absent from the export, everything absent from here is in it.
+    'api', 'assets', 'cats', 'fonts', 'hero', 'images', 'knet', 'pay',
+    // Never writable from an artifact, whoever owns them.
+    'storage', 'cgi-bin', '.well-known', 'config.php',
+    // NOT protected, and each on purpose: 'admin', 'queue' and 'orders' are
+    // static routes the site publishes, and '.htaccess' is the site's own —
+    // protecting them refused every deploy this endpoint was built to serve.
 ];

-const ALLOWED_HOSTS  = ['raw.githubusercontent.com', 'github.com', 'codeload.github.com'];
+/**
+ * Default artifact hosts. Add others in <domain>/storage/deploy.hosts, one
+ * hostname per line, blanks and #comments ignored — a file rather than an edit
+ * here, so changing where artifacts live does not mean re-patching this
+ * endpoint and does not put the list inside public_html.
+ */
+const ALLOWED_HOSTS  = ['raw.githubusercontent.com', 'github.com', 'codeload.github.com'];
 const MAX_BYTES      = 200 * 1024 * 1024;
 const KEEP_RELEASES  = 3;
@@
 function isProtected(string $rel): bool {
     $first = explode('/', str_replace('\\', '/', $rel))[0];
     return in_array($first, PROTECTED_PATHS, true);
 }
+
+function allowedHosts(): array {
+    global $STORAGE;
+    $hosts = ALLOWED_HOSTS;
+    $file  = $STORAGE . '/deploy.hosts';
+    if (is_readable($file)) {
+        foreach (preg_split('/\R/', (string) file_get_contents($file)) ?: [] as $line) {
+            $line = trim($line);
+            if ($line === '' || $line[0] === '#') continue;
+            // A hostname only. A URL, a port or a path here would silently
+            // widen the check to something that never matches parse_url's host.
+            if (preg_match('/^[a-z0-9.-]+$/i', $line)) $hosts[] = strtolower($line);
+        }
+    }
+    return array_values(array_unique($hosts));
+}
@@
 $host = parse_url($url, PHP_URL_HOST) ?: '';
-if (parse_url($url, PHP_URL_SCHEME) !== 'https' || !in_array($host, ALLOWED_HOSTS, true)) {
+if (parse_url($url, PHP_URL_SCHEME) !== 'https' || !in_array(strtolower($host), allowedHosts(), true)) {
     out(400, ['ok' => false, 'error' => 'host_not_allowed', 'host' => $host]);
 }


AFTERWARDS

  1. Add the artifact host, if it is not GitHub:

       printf '%s\n' files.example.com > ~/domains/wainkw.com/storage/deploy.hosts
       chmod 600 ~/domains/wainkw.com/storage/deploy.hosts

  2. Check it still parses before trusting it:

       php -l public_html/api/deploy.php

  3. First deploy through it should be watched. `storage/deploy/deploy.log`
     records one line per attempt, and `storage/deploy/manifest.json` is what
     the prune step reads next time — an empty or wrong manifest on the first
     run is the case worth catching, because it decides what gets deleted on
     the second.

  4. Nothing here has been tested against the live endpoint. It cannot be from
     this environment; the site is unreachable at CONNECT. The patch is a
     reading of the file plus a measurement of the docroot, and it should be
     treated as a proposal until `php -l` passes and one deploy has run.
